# The tenure block sharing that took runbench's phase from forty-nine blocks to
# one. This row gated on the run program's counters until the lap carry, which
# stages runbench's inner loop so rarely that one block holds its tenure with
# or without the sharing, and the mutation left that gate green. The mem
# fixture an_inner_beat_opens_its_tenure_in_the_block_outside still opens a
# block per inner beat without it, and goes red.
ten_open = row
  "an inner beat that opens its own tenure block beside one with room"
  true
  "cargo test --release --test golden mem_corpus_pins_native_allocator_counters"
  "specs (unit, golden, differential)"
  (breaking "a_child_beat_that_opens_its_own_tenure_block")
  release

# Where a tenure block dies. `ten_blocks` and `ten_frees` count the block being
# claimed and given back and neither can say at which depth, so freeing a
# depth's blocks instead of handing them up moved no counter at all until
# `ten_handups` was minted on 2026-09-22. The fixture that documented the
# hand-up had claimed for a fortnight to guard it.
ten_where = row
  "a tenure block freed at the depth that opened it instead of handed up"
  true
  "cargo test --release --test golden mem_corpus_pins_native_allocator_counters"
  "specs (unit, golden, differential)"
  (breaking "a_tenure_block_freed_where_it_was_handed_up")
  release

# Which block a pointer lies in. Past eight blocks the survival questions are
